{"maxId":135,"entries":{"chapter-58-study-delirium|Chapter 58 Study: Delirium":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Delirium = acute disorder of attention + global cognition; affects as many as half of hospitalized adults 65+ (p879).":["u3"],"copy|65+ patients account for ~40% of all hospital days; costs >$60,000/patient, $183B/year nationally (2018 USD) (p879).":["u4"],"copy|Up to 50% of cases are preventable (p879).":["u5"],"copy|Clinicians fail to detect up to 70–85% of cases (p880).":["u6"],"copy|ICU incidence 16–82%; delirium occurs in up to 48% in nursing home/postacute settings and up to 83% at end of life (p880).":["u7"],"copy|ICU delirium → 4× in-hospital mortality, 6× mortality at 6 months (p880).":["u8"],"copy|DSM-5: attention/awareness disturbance + acute/fluctuating onset + added cognitive deficit + not better explained by dementia and not in the context of severely reduced consciousness/coma + medical etiology (p879–880).":["u9"],"copy|CAM (4-item algorithm: acute onset/fluctuating course + inattention + [disorganized thinking OR altered consciousness]) — sensitivity 94–100%, specificity 90–95% (p880).":["u10"],"copy|Cardinal features: acute onset hours–days, fluctuating course, inattention — vs dementia's insidious weeks–months course (p883).":["u11"],"copy|Hypoactive form is the more common form in older patients; it often goes unrecognized and carries an overall poorer prognosis (p883).":["u12"],"copy|Screening tools (Table 58-3, p884): 4AT scores 0–12, ≥4 suggests delirium; Nu-DESC scores 0–10; Current practice (not the book): when delirium indicators are present, a competent practitioner should assess with 4AT; use CAM-ICU or ICDSC in critical care or the recovery room after surgery; a healthcare professional with relevant expertise makes the final diagnosis if assessment indicates delirium - NICE CG103, recommendations updated 2023, 1.6.1-1.6.2, p11":["u13"],"copy|Predisposing: dementia (2–5× risk, present in up to two-thirds of delirium cases), age >70, severe illness/multimorbidity, functional/sensory impairment, prior delirium or stroke (p881).":["u14"],"copy|Precipitating (Table 58-1, p882): polypharmacy, restraints, bladder catheter, infection, iatrogenic complications, surgery, urgent/trauma admission, coma, ICU stay >10 days, and physiologic derangements (elevated BUN/creatinine ratio, abnormal Na/glucose/K, metabolic acidosis).":["u15"],"copy|Validated hospital precipitants (restraints, malnutrition, >3 medications added in the previous day, bladder catheter, any iatrogenic event): each independently confers 2–4× risk; effects are cumulative (p882).":["u16"],"copy|Medications contribute to ≥30% of cases (p882). Any psychoactive drug 4× risk; ≥2 psychoactive drugs 5× risk; sedative-hypnotics 3–12×, narcotics 3×, anticholinergics 5–12× (p882).":["u17"],"copy|Beers-listed culprits (Table 58-2, p883): anticholinergics, antipsychotics, benzodiazepines, corticosteroids, H2-blockers, meperidine, Z-drugs (zolpidem/eszopiclone/zaleplon), TCAs. Psychoactive drugs should be discontinued or minimized whenever possible (p885); Required reading: see AGS Beers 2023 required-article card for opioids and delirium, including the balanced pain-management caveat (Table 3, p2066)":["u18"],"copy|CAM short form applied in over 10,000 studies, translated into over 19 languages (p884).":["u19"],"copy|As few as 20% attain complete symptom resolution at 6 months; cognition can be affected for up to 1 year (p884).":["u20"],"copy|LP/brain imaging/EEG probably indicated in fewer than 5–10% of cases — LP for the febrile patient with suspected meningitis/encephalitis; imaging for new focal signs, head trauma, or no other identifiable cause (p885). EEG false-negative 17%, false-positive 22% (p885).":["u21"],"copy|Differential: delirium vs dementia vs depression vs acute psychosis (Table 58-4, p886) — level of consciousness (altered vs normal) separates delirium from acute psychosis (2026 Q14 key); attention differs too (impaired vs variable) but overlaps and doesn't separate as cleanly.":["u22"],"copy|Targeted multicomponent prevention (Table 58-5 risk factors) vs standard care → 40% risk reduction in hospitalized older patients (p886).":["u23"],"copy|Proactive geriatric consultation post-hip-fracture → 40% reduction (p887).":["u24"],"copy|Nonpharmacologic strategies are first-line for every patient: reorientation, mobilize early, correct vision/hearing, avoid restraints, protect sleep, treat dehydration early (Table 58-5, p888).":["u25"],"copy|Nonpharm sleep protocol (warm milk/tea + relaxation music + back massage) cut sleep-med use 54% → 31% (p889).":["u26"],"copy|Antipsychotics = last resort, for safety/symptom control only: Haloperidol — start 0.25 mg PO/parenteral, may repeat q30min after rechecking vitals; most antipsychotic-naïve older patients should need a total loading dose of no more than 2.5 mg; maintenance = half the loading dose in divided doses over the next 24 h, tapered over the ensuing 48 h. Caution: EPS/dystonia; avoid in Parkinson disease/Lewy body dementia; IV only in monitored settings (torsades risk) (p889). Quetiapine — start 12.5 mg, 24-h max 25 mg (p889). Olanzapine — start 2.5 mg, 24-h max 10 mg (p889). Risperidone — start 0.25–0.5 mg, 24-h max 1.5 mg (p889). Official warnings: increased mortality with haloperidol and atypical antipsychotics in patients with dementia (p889).":["u27"],"copy|Benzodiazepines: avoid as first-line (oversedation, worsens delirium); remain treatment of choice for delirium from seizures or alcohol/medication withdrawal (p889).":["u28"],"copy|Dexmedetomidine: shortens delirium duration/ICU stay in ventilated patients; useful if haloperidol-resistant (p889).":["u29"],"copy|COVID-19: hospitalization delirium rates 25–84% (p889).":["u30"],"copy|Postacute/nursing home: 16% meet full CAM criteria on admission, another 50% are subsyndromal; of those admitted with delirium, >50% still delirious at 1 month (p890).":["u31"],"copy|End of life: delirium in >80% of patients; if sedation is indicated for severe agitated delirium, lorazepam, starting dose 0.5–1.0 mg PO/IV/SQ is the sedation agent of choice (p890).":["u32"],"copy|Acute-onset timeframe: Cardinal Features/Table 58-4 say \"hours to days,\" but the Algorithm-for-Evaluation text says \"hours to weeks\" for acute change — both as printed, not reconciled; a 2–3-week history falls inside one window and outside the other (p886).":["u33"],"copy|Figure 58-2's flowchart ends \"avoid benzodiazepines and other psychoactive drugs\" and gives only starting doses; the body text on p889 adds that benzodiazepines remain the treatment of choice for seizure/withdrawal delirium and gives the 24-h antipsychotic maximums the figure omits (p887).":["u34"],"copy|Risperidone dose: Figure 58-2 gives 0.25 mg; the Antipsychotics text gives 0.25–0.5 mg — both as printed (p889).":["u35"],"recall|Recall":["u36"],"2026-exam-questions-for-this-chapter|2026 exam questions for this chapter":["u37"],"copy|א. Presence of delusions":["u38"],"copy|ב. Acute onset":["u39"],"copy|ג. Impaired attention":["u40"],"copy|ד. Changing level of consciousness ← key":["u41"],"copy|א. Benzodiazepines as first line, to secure sedation and prevent line removal":["u42"],"copy|ב. IV antipsychotic (haloperidol), with slow dose titration until effect, to avoid restraint ← key":["u43"],"copy|ג. Physical restraint before any drug, to avoid drug side effects":["u44"],"copy|ד. IV dexmedetomidine (Precedex) to shorten the delirium ← key":["u45"],"copy|א. Delirium cannot be diagnosed because there is no clear reversible cause":["u46"],"copy|ב. This is delirium even in the absence of a clear etiology ← key":["u47"],"copy|ג. Delirium cannot be diagnosed without evidence of a change in level of consciousness":["u48"],"copy|ד. This is most likely a hidden dementia that worsened during the admission":["u49"],"copy|א. Delirium is really an early stage of previously undiagnosed dementia":["u50"],"copy|ב. Dementia is a major risk factor for delirium, but delirium itself does not affect the risk of future cognitive decline":["u51"],"copy|ג. Delirium causes permanent brain damage and therefore necessarily leads to dementia":["u52"],"copy|ד. Delirium is associated with a higher risk of cognitive decline and dementia, even in people without prior dementia ← key":["u53"],"copy|א. They may be used as additional therapy in extreme agitation that has not responded to other treatment ← key":["u54"],"copy|ב. They should be avoided because they raise the anticholinergic burden":["u55"],"copy|ג. They may be used only if the patient has no background dementia":["u56"],"copy|ד. They are the preferred first-line treatment, before haloperidol":["u57"],"copy|א. CAM negative, 4AT ≥ 4":["u58"],"copy|ב. CAM positive, 4AT < 4":["u59"],"copy|ג. CAM positive, 4AT ≥ 4 ← key":["u60"],"copy|ד. CAM negative, 4AT < 4":["u61"],"copy|CHAPTER 58s / HAZZARD":["u62"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 58 — Delirium; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u63"],"copy|Definition & epidemiology":["u64"],"copy|Diagnosis":["u65"],"copy|Risk factors":["u66"],"copy|Drugs & delirium":["u67"],"copy|Evaluation":["u68"],"copy|Prevention":["u69"],"copy|Management":["u70"],"copy|Special populations":["u71"],"copy|Exam traps / book contradictions":["u72"],"copy|1.":["u73"],"copy|What acute disorder of attention and cognition affects up to half of hospitalized patients 65 and older, and what fraction of cases are preventable?":["u74"],"copy|Delirium; up to 50% of cases are preventable (p879).":["u75"],"copy|2.":["u76"],"copy|Name the four elements of the CAM diagnostic algorithm.":["u77"],"copy|Acute onset and fluctuating course, plus inattention, plus either disorganized thinking or altered level of consciousness (p880).":["u78"],"copy|3.":["u79"],"copy|What are the CAM's sensitivity and specificity?":["u80"],"copy|Sensitivity 94–100%, specificity 90–95% (p880).":["u81"],"copy|5.":["u82"],"copy|What predisposing factor is present in up to two-thirds of delirium cases, and what relative risk does it confer?":["u83"],"copy|Dementia; confers a 2- to 5-fold increased risk of delirium (p881).":["u84"],"copy|6.":["u85"],"copy|Name three precipitating factors validated in delirium prediction models besides medications.":["u86"],"copy|Any three of: physical restraints, indwelling bladder catheter, malnutrition, iatrogenic event, urgent/trauma admission, ICU stay >10 days, coma (p882).":["u87"],"copy|9.":["u88"],"copy|Which form of delirium (hypoactive, hyperactive, or mixed) is the more common form in older patients, and what does the book say about its recognition and prognosis?":["u89"],"copy|Hypoactive — it often goes unrecognized (reduced activity is often attributed to low mood or fatigue) and carries an overall poorer prognosis (p883).":["u90"],"copy|12.":["u91"],"copy|What haloperidol starting dose is recommended for severe delirium agitation, and what total loading dose should most antipsychotic-naïve older patients need?":["u92"],"copy|Start 0.25 mg orally or parenterally, repeatable every 30 minutes after vital signs are rechecked; most should require a total loading dose of no more than 2.5 mg (p889).":["u93"],"copy|14.":["u94"],"copy|In postacute/nursing-home admissions, what fraction of patients meet full CAM criteria for delirium at admission, and what fraction of those admitted with delirium are still delirious a month later?":["u95"],"copy|16% meet full CAM criteria on admission; of those with delirium, over 50% are still delirious 1 month later (p890).":["u96"],"copy|15.":["u97"],"copy|What agent and starting dose are recommended for sedation in severe agitated delirium at the end of life?":["u98"],"copy|Lorazepam, starting dose 0.5–1.0 mg PO, IV, or SQ (p890).":["u99"],"copy|The actual 2026 specialist-exam questions and answer keys sourced to this chapter, reproduced verbatim as printed in the full chapter file.":["u100"],"copy|Q14 — sourced to [p.":["u101"],"copy|886, Table 58-4].":["u102"],"copy|Which feature best distinguishes delirium from acute psychosis?":["u103"],"copy|Q27 — sourced to [p.":["u104"],"copy|889].":["u105","u119"],"copy|An 80-year-old man with acute delirium in the ICU after a brain hemorrhage; BP 180/90.":["u106"],"copy|He is extremely restless, tries to pull out his breathing tube and central lines, and endangers his safety and continued care.":["u107"],"copy|What is the most appropriate therapeutic approach at this stage?":["u108"],"copy|Q34 — sourced to [p.":["u109"],"copy|879–881].":["u110"],"copy|An 87-year-old man admitted for worsening heart failure; background hypertension and mild CKD, no previous dementia diagnosis.":["u111"],"copy|During the admission: reduced concentration, psychomotor slowing with slow responses, daytime sleepiness, more alert at night, sometimes wrong about place and time, memory varying between examinations.":["u112"],"copy|No fever, no clear infection, no focal neurological deficit, normal brain CT.":["u113"],"copy|Which statement is most accurate?":["u114"],"copy|Q40 (support p883-884) — sourced to [p.":["u115"],"copy|880–882].":["u116"],"copy|What is the relationship between delirium and dementia?":["u117"],"copy|Q58 (support p890) — sourced to [p.":["u118"],"copy|In a patient with delirium at the end of life, what is the recommendation on benzodiazepines?":["u120"],"copy|Q74 — sourced to [p.":["u121"],"copy|884, Table 58-3].":["u122"],"copy|An 84-year-old man with mild Alzheimer-type dementia, living at home, admitted with pneumonia.":["u123"],"copy|On day 2 of the admission the nurse reports that he has suddenly become very confused, cannot focus in conversation, and believes he is in a hotel.":["u124"],"copy|On examination he has difficulty reciting the months of the year backwards; his state is noted to improve in the mornings and worsen in the evenings.":["u125"],"copy|Given these data, what are the likely CAM Short Form and 4AT results?":["u126"],"copy|Answer":["u127","